<sn><md>#### Why exclude .git and .gitignore?

- **`.git/`** holds your entire commit history, branches, and sometimes things you committed and later removed. On a public web server it can expose source code and secrets, and it bloats the upload with thousands of tiny files, which is slow on free hosting.
- **`.gitignore`** is only meaningful to Git. It has no job on the server and can leak hints about your project structure.

Neither belongs in `htdocs`, so every method below filters them out.
</md></sn><sn><md>### What you need from InfinityFree

Open your [InfinityFree client area](https://dash.infinityfree.com/accounts/),select ++Hosting Account++ Tab, note the FTP details in the right side:

![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-em0n/QDoc/main/Programming/rclone/02.Configuring%20Cloud%20Storage/assets/infinity_account_screenshot.png)

| Setting | Typical value |
| --- | --- |
| FTP host | `ftpupload.net` (use the hostname shown in your account) |
| Username | something like `if0_12345678` or `epiz_12345678` |
| Password | your hosting account password |
| Port | `21` default |
| Remote folder | `htdocs` (your site's public root) |

Two practical notes:

- InfinityFree offers plain FTP. Never reuse that password elsewhere, and don't commit it to your repository.
- Always upload into `htdocs` (or the folder of the domain you are deploying). Files placed outside it are not served.

</md></sn><sn><md>### rclone 

best one-command sync

[rclone](https://rclone.org) is a cross-platform command-line tool that supports FTP and has powerful include/exclude filters.

#### Create the FTP remote

```bash
rclone config
```

Choose `n` (new remote), name it `infinity`, pick the `ftp` storage type (may be `18` number in the given list), and enter:

- host: `ftpupload.net`
- user: your InfinityFree FTP username
- port: `21`
- password: your FTP password (rclone stores it obscured)

Test the connection:

```bash
rclone lsd infinity:
```

You should see `htdocs` listed.
</md></sn><sn><md>### Do a dry run first

From the root of your local repository: Open Terminal then run

```bash
rclone sync . infinity:htdocs \
  --exclude ".git/**" \
  --exclude ".gitignore" \
  --transfers 2 --checkers 2 \
  --dry-run -v
```

What each part does:

- `sync` makes the remote match the local folder, so files deleted locally are **deleted on the server**. Use `rclone copy` instead if you never want deletions.
- `--exclude ".git/**"` skips the `.git` directory and everything under it, at any depth.
- `--exclude ".gitignore"` skips any file named `.gitignore`. Use `/.gitignore` to exclude only the one in the repository root.
- `--transfers 2 --checkers 2` keeps connections low. Free hosts often reject too many parallel FTP connections.
- `--dry-run` shows what would happen without changing anything.
</md></sn><sn><md>### Run it for real

Remove `--dry-run`:

```bash
rclone sync . infinity:htdocs --exclude ".git/**" --exclude ".gitignore" --transfers 2 --checkers 2 -v
```

If every run re-uploads all files, the server may not report modification times reliably. Add `--size-only` so rclone compares by size instead.


##### Optional: keep filters in a file

Create `ftp-filter.txt` (keep it out of the upload as well):

```text
- .git/**
- .gitignore
- ftp-filter.txt
- README.md
+ **
```

Then run:

```bash
rclone sync . infinity:htdocs --filter-from ftp-filter.txt --transfers 2 --checkers 2 -v
```

Rules are checked top to bottom and the first match wins, so the final `+ **` includes everything not already excluded.
</md></sn><sn><md>### Make it a one-command habit

Wrap your chosen command in a tiny script at the root of the repository.

`deploy.sh`:

```bash
#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -euo pipefail

rclone sync . infinity:htdocs \
  --exclude ".git/**" --exclude ".gitignore" \
  --exclude "deploy.sh" \
  --transfers 2 --checkers 2 -v
```

Now your routine is simply:

```bash
git pull
./deploy.sh
```
